  "final_audio_transcript": " This bumblebee is rolling a wooden ball.  There is no nectar inside.  So why does it keep rolling?  In a laboratory, researchers gave bees a clear route  to food, with wooden balls placed off to the sides.  The bees could walk straight past,  but many stopped, climbed onto movable balls,  and rolled them anyway.  Some rolled again and again, even after feeding.  The balls offered no sugar or pollen reward.  In a separate test, young bees rolled more often  than older bees that were equally new to the balls.  Another test offered different colored chambers.  Bees later chose more often the color linked  to earlier ball rolling.  That suggests the activity itself was rewarding,  even without an edible prize.  The researchers called it play-like behavior.  Yet a laboratory test cannot directly reveal  how bees feel or how often wild bees do this.  An insect rolling, a ball may be showing us  that play has more forms than we usually notice.",
  "final_script_similarity": 0.9813664596273292,
